[32m+[m[32m# Guide: RSA Common Modulus Attack[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## Prerequisites[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mTo understand this attack, you need familiarity with:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m1. **RSA Basics:** $c \equiv m^e \pmod N$[m
[32m+[m[32m2. **Greatest Common Divisor (GCD):**[m[41m [m
[32m+[m[32m3. **Bézout's Identity:** If $\gcd(x, y) = 1$, there exist integers $a$ and $b$ such that $ax + by = 1$[m
[32m+[m[32m4. **Modular Inverse:** Calculating $x^{-1} \pmod N$[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m## Theory[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mWe possess two equations:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m1. $c_1 \equiv m^{e_1} \pmod N$[m
[32m+[m[32m2. $c_2 \equiv m^{e_2} \pmod N$[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mIf the two exponents $e_1$ and $e_2$ are coprime (i.e., $\gcd(e_1, e_2) = 1$), the Extended Euclidean Algorithm guarantees that we can find two integers, $a$ and $b$, satisfying:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m$$a \cdot e_1 + b \cdot e_2 = 1$$[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mSince we know $c_1$ and $c_2$ are just powers of $m$, we can raise them to the powers of $a$ and $b$ respectively and multiply them:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m$$(c_1)^a \cdot (c_2)^b \equiv (m^{e_1})^a \cdot (m^{e_2})^b \pmod N$$[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mBy exponent laws, this simplifies to:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m$$m^{a \cdot e_1} \cdot m^{b \cdot e_2} \equiv m^{(a \cdot e_1 + b \cdot e_2)} \pmod N$$[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mSubstituting our identity ($ae_1 + be_2 = 1$):[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m$$m^1 \equiv m \pmod N$$[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m**Conclusion:** We recover $m$ directly.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m### Handling Negative Coefficients[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mIf your coefficient $a$ is negative:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m1. Calculate the modular inverse of the ciphertext: $\text{inv} = c_1^{-1} \pmod N$[m
[32m+[m[32m2. Raise this inverse to the absolute value of $a$: $\text{inv}^{|a|} \pmod N$[m
[32m+[m
